Facebook is under fire again this  morning for how it handled evidence of  Russian election interference on its  site scathing New York Times report  based on interviews with more than 50  people shines a harsh light on  Facebook's two top executives it says  CEO Mark Zuckerberg and CEO of Sheryl  Sandberg ignored warning signs and then  sought to conceal them from public view  The Times report security experts at  Facebook flagged attempts by Russian  hackers to probe accounts as early as  spring 2016 that's months before  Zuckerberg downplayed the effect of  Russian propaganda on the 2016 election  it also said Sandberg oversaw an  aggressive campaign to combat Facebook's  critics in response to the article  Facebook told us we are working hard to  ensure that we protect our community  from bad actors CBS News contributor  Nicholas Thompson is Wired's  editor-in-chief and he joins us with  more on this expose Nick great to see  you a big bombshell from the New York  Times Facebook already responding by  saying that it's already ended its  relationship with that consulting firm  that was disparaging those who were  speaking out against Facebook is that  enough or do we need to see more of a  change specifically leadership well that  was a very small step they ended one  relationship with one company doing one  set of small things that a lot of people  were critical of so is that enough to  change it of course it's not enough to  change the narrative but the question  you're asked is exactly the right one is  there gonna have to be a big change in  leadership at Facebook my instinct is  probably not people still trust Sheryl  they still trust mark by by and large  they're very well respected by the board  which is the most important constituency  so I don't think they're going anywhere  so from a creditability standpoint where  does that leave facebook users Facebook  has a big credibility problem they've  had a growing credibility problem over  the last two years this story definitely  does not help the issue is is that in  2016 Mark Zuckerberg publicly said it's  crazy to think that they influence the  election or that there was Russian  involvement but the real story is that  inside Facebook they knew about Russian  involvement well okay so this is super  complicated to untangle so what he said  is that fake news didn't have a big  impact on the election at the time they  knew about some of the fake news and  they knew about Russian military  operations hacking Opera  the big question which isn't really  answered in The Times story is whether  they knew about the Russian propaganda  pages the fake pages and all those fake  ads if they knew about them then which  isn't what the time says then it would  say that they've been lying massively  the other question I don't think is  answered is related to Cambridge  analytical in the back door right that  Facebook allowed many of these whether  it was Russians or other advertising  companies access to personal security  information yeah so that is that is a  huge question and there's no question  Facebook didn't do it on purpose  remember Cambridge analytical was a big  disaster for the company the question is  whether Facebook knew about the problem  and should have done more previously  they basically said though very publicly  mark was on Capitol Hill it sort of had  a public flogging they've said listen  we've screwed up we made a mistake we  should have done more we should have  done better yeah what do you think needs  to happen now well okay so them to  really get out of this because the  question yeah biggest question for the  company and something that times has  been reporting on but it isn't in this  story is as the company fundamentally  changed do they take propaganda much  more seriously are they working much  harder to protect your privacy there's a  lot going on at the company right now I  think they're making steps we don't know  if they've done enough and for now you  think management is safe management is  safe Nick Thompson thank you so much you
 

